  "body": "HTTPClient marks a contract: a declaration with no implementation, like a Spring @HttpExchange interface. Tachyon generates each request, the constructor and the behavior context. Declare the client in the bodiless form of your language: an interface in Java, Groovy, Kotlin, C# and PHP, a Go interface, an abstract interface class in Dart, a trait in Rust, a type with only abstract members in F#, and a Protocol class whose methods end with ... in Python. JavaScript, TypeScript and Ruby have no method without a body that takes annotations, so there the client is a class whose methods have empty bodies. A method body, a field or a constructor in a client is a build error (TY1308), and `ty migrate fix --write` rewrites a class client. Put logic that works on the answer into a service that needs the client. HTTPClient can take a path that comes before every method path, like HTTPClient(\"/api\"). It can also take an object with path, adapter, cache and setup. The adapter is the code that really sends the request. Its default name is the class name without Client, in lowerCamel case. So CatalogClient uses the catalog adapter. The setup is the Setup class that registers the adapter. Its default name is the class name with Setup instead of Client, so CatalogClient uses CatalogSetup, and this example needs no option. GetRequest(\"/products\") on a method sends a GET request to /products. PostRequest, PutRequest, PatchRequest and DeleteRequest send the other HTTP methods. A marker on each parameter says where its value goes. PathVariable fills a {name} part of the path. RequestParam(\"page\") is a value for the query string, after ?. RequestHeader(\"X-Tenant\") is a value for a request header. RequestBody is the JSON body of the request. Tachyon builds the request from the arguments. The adapter holds the base origin (the scheme, host and port) and sends the request. The path is relative to that origin. Tachyon refuses headers that the transport controls. GET and HEAD have no body. The answer is JSON of limited size. RestClient makes the same adapter call from normal code, without an annotation. Use it when you know the shape of the request only at call time. RestClient is a method of the behavior context. In JavaScript, `this.behaviors.restClient(\"catalog\").get(\"/products\").query({ page: 1 }).header(\"X-Tenant\", \"acme\").retrieve()` sends GET /products?page=1 through the catalog adapter. The builder also has post, put, patch, delete, head and options, and body sets the JSON body. GET and HEAD refuse a body. Python, Ruby and Rust write `rest_client`, and Go writes `RestClient`, which also returns an error. In Rust and Go, query takes one name and one value. retrieve sends the call with the same limits as an HTTPClient method. See the [routing annotation glossary](/docs/routing-annotations) for each name. Lua has no tab here, because the build refuses HTTPClient in Lua Yon code with TY1308: Lua gets gateway clients in a later step (ADR 0085).\n\nC++ has no tab here, because C++ Yon code has only route controllers so far: the build refuses `HTTPClient` in C++ with `TY1308` (ADR 0099)."
